When reading from a streamed body, it is not possible to timeout. As a result, the client can get stuck trying to read the body. It can happen in many occasions, for instance if the remote end crashes or just forgets about you, the client will try to read from the socket forever (c.f. half-open tcp).

Example:

```
$client = new Client([
    'timeout' => 300,
    'stream' => true,
]);

$response = $client->get('http://example.com/');

$body = $response->getBody()->detach();

while (!feof($body)) {
    // this can hang forever
    $line = fgets($body);
}
```

I would be great if Guzzle allowed to specify a read timeout. I didn't find a way to apply a timeout myself.

Here is a code describing a problem I got:

``` php

$big_data = str_repeat('a', 1000000);

try {
    $body = stream_for($big_data);
    $signature = md5($body); // I have to read whole body to build signature...
    // $body->rewind();

    $client = new Client();

    $response = $client->post('http://httpbin.org/post', ["body" => $body]);

    echo json_decode($response->getBody())->data, PHP_EOL;
}
catch (Exception $e) {
    echo (string) $e->getMessage(), PHP_EOL;
}
```

This code results in curl timeout. Since I consumed body stream already and size of body more then 1000000, CurlHandler uses CURLOPT_READFUNCTION https://github.com/guzzle/guzzle/blob/2e5c595512de4885435eaff24b67066769b64b34/src/Handler/CurlFactory.php#L268 to read body. Since body is EOF currently, read returns empty string and curl just hangs until timeout reached.
I am ok with rewinding body myself, but in case when body length is less then 1000000, then stream will be casted to string which implicitly calls rewind on the stream making request magically work. 
StreamHandler also casting body to a string, so it works as well.

My guess here is that CurlHandler should rewind stream before consuming, since Content-length is calculated based on full stream length and in case we consumed chunk of the stream it would produce broken request.

I have a use case where a service object uses the Guzzle client to send several parallel HTTP requests. If any of those requests fail, I want to throw an exception which the caller will handle, and disregard any other responses that follow.

The [sending requests in parallel](http://guzzle.readthedocs.org/en/latest/clients.html#sending-requests-in-parallel) documentation says, under the [throwing exceptions immediately](http://guzzle.readthedocs.org/en/latest/clients.html#throwing-errors-immediately) section:

> The following example shows how to use an event listener to throw exceptions immeditaley and prevent subsequent requests from being sent.

Great! That's exactly what I want... and here's some sample code where I try to implement it:

``` php
<?php

use GuzzleHttp\Client;
use GuzzleHttp\Event\CompleteEvent;
use GuzzleHttp\Event\ErrorEvent;

require __DIR__ . '/../vendor/autoload.php';

$client = new Client();
$requests = [
    $client->createRequest('GET', 'http://thisdomaindoesntreallyexistsoihopetherequesterrors.com'),
    $client->createRequest('GET', 'http://google.com'),
];

echo "Sending requests in parallel...\n\n";

try {

    $client->sendAll(
        $requests,
        [
            'complete' => function (CompleteEvent $event) {
                echo 'COMPLETE: ' . $event->getRequest()->getUrl() .  ")\n\n";
            },
            'error' => function (ErrorEvent $event) {
                $exception = $event->getException();
                echo 'ERROR: ' . $event->getRequest()->getUrl() . ")\n" .
                     'I am throwing a ' . get_class($exception) . " now!\n\n";
                throw $exception;
            }
        ]
    );
} catch (\Exception $e) {
    echo "Caught the following exception:\n";
    echo $e->getTraceAsString();
    exit;
}

echo "The exception wasn't caught. :(\n";
```

What I _expect_ to see is this:

```
Sending requests in parallel...

ERROR: http://thisdomaindoesntreallyexistsoihopetherequesterrors.com)
I am throwing a GuzzleHttp\Exception\RequestException now!

Caught the following exception:
<exception trace goes here>
```

but what I _actually_ see is this:

```
Sending requests in parallel...

ERROR: http://thisdomaindoesntreallyexistsoihopetherequesterrors.com)
I am throwing a GuzzleHttp\Exception\RequestException now!

COMPLETE: http://www.google.com/)

The exception wasn't caught. :(
```

I did some digging and tracked the issue down to [here](https://github.com/guzzle/guzzle/blob/4.1.6/src/Adapter/Curl/MultiAdapter.php#L243-L249). The `MultiAdapter` will only throw a caught exception if the `BatchContext` allows it.

It seems that the `MultiAdapter` creates a `BatchContext` that allows exceptions to be thrown when using [->send(...)](https://github.com/guzzle/guzzle/blob/4.1.6/src/Adapter/Curl/MultiAdapter.php#L101), but not when using [->sendAll(...)](https://github.com/guzzle/guzzle/blob/4.1.6/src/Adapter/Curl/MultiAdapter.php#L112), nor does it appear to be configurable.

Am I misunderstanding the documentation with regard to how this is supposed to work? Or is this a bug?

**Guzzle version(s) affected**: 7.0.1, 6.5.5
**PHP version**: PHP 7.4.8 (x64, VC15, NTS)
**cURL version**: cURL 7.70.0

**Description**  

When reading the response body stream using a loop similar to:

```php
	while (true) {
		$eof = $response->getBody()->eof();

		if ($eof) break;

		$chunk = $response->getBody()->read(4096);
	}
```

the last chunk will be missing if the response length is between `$n * 4096 + 1` (inclusive)  and `($n + 1) * 4096` (exclusive) with `$n` being an even integer. In other words: If the last chunk were in the second half of a 8kB block or if reading the full file requires an even number of iterations.

The response would appear to be truncated at exactly a 4096 Byte boundary.

**How to reproduce**  

Running the following example script with `SHOW_BUG` on Windows will print `BUG FOR SIZE` for sizes 4097, 8191, 12289, 16383. 

```php
<?php
const SHOW_BUG = true;

require('vendor/autoload.php');

$sizes = [ 4095, 4096, 4097, 8191, 8192, 8193, 12287, 12288, 12289, 16383, 16384, 16385 ];

$client = new \GuzzleHttp\Client();
foreach ($sizes as $size) {
	printf("Downloading size(%d)\n", $size);

	if (SHOW_BUG) {
		$options = [
		];
	} else {
		$options = [
			'sink' => \GuzzleHttp\Psr7\stream_for(fopen("php://temp", "w+"))
		];
	}

	$response = $client->get('https://gist.githubusercontent.com/TimWolla/1c56cbb9924e2fdf13b5cd1597ac981f/raw/02885d2eab708619d8a24a77155a47e93453ea2f/'.$size.'.txt', $options);
	$i = 0; $total = 0;
	while (true) {
		printf("Iteration %d: ", ++$i);

		$eof = $response->getBody()->eof();
		printf("EOF(%s) ", var_export($eof, true));

		if ($eof) break;

		$chunk = $response->getBody()->read(4096);
		$len = strlen($chunk);
		$total += $len;
		printf("bytes(%d) total(%d)", $len, $total);
		echo "\n";
	}
	echo "\n";
	if ($total !== $size) {
		printf("BUG FOR SIZE %d\n", $size);
	}

	echo "\n";
}
```

The bug can also be reproduced using a simplified example only relying on `curl`:

```php
<?php
const SHOW_BUG = true;

if (SHOW_BUG) {
	$filename = "php://temp";
}
else {
	$filename = __DIR__."/temp";
}

$sizes = [ 4095, 4096, 4097, 8191, 8192, 8193, 12287, 12288, 12289, 16383, 16384, 16385 ];

foreach ($sizes as $size) {
	printf("Downloading size(%d)\n", $size);

	$ch = curl_init();
	curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_URL, 'https://gist.githubusercontent.com/TimWolla/1c56cbb9924e2fdf13b5cd1597ac981f/raw/02885d2eab708619d8a24a77155a47e93453ea2f/'.$size.'.txt');
	curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_FILE, $f = fopen($filename, "w+"));
	curl_exec($ch);
	curl_close($ch);

	fseek($f, 0);

	$i = 0; $total = 0;
	while (true) {
		printf("Iteration %d: ", ++$i);
		
		$eof = feof($f);
		printf("EOF(%s) ", var_export($eof, true));

		if ($eof) break;

		$chunk = fread($f, 4096);
		$len = strlen($chunk);
		$total += $len;
		printf("bytes(%d) total(%d)", $len, $total);
		echo "\n";
	}
	echo "\n";
	if ($total !== $size) {
		printf("BUG FOR SIZE %d\n", $size);
	}

	echo "\n";
}
```

You can find a complete reproducer environment within the following guzzle.tar.gz: [guzzle.tar.gz](https://github.com/guzzle/guzzle/files/4953004/guzzle.tar.gz). `test.php` is the Guzzle based reproducer. `test2.php` is the raw curl based reproducer.

Example output of `test.php`:

```

