**Possible Solution**  

My understanding of this bug is that directly writing into a `php://temp` stream using `CURLOPT_FILE` is unsafe on Windows. It appears as if PHP does not correctly recognize the stream size increasing there.

This issue can be worked around by always using `CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION` to manually write into the stream (as used when a `sink` is explicitely specified). Another alternative would be using the [`tmpfile()`](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.tmpfile.php) function, which always creates a filesystem backed file.

This most likely is a bug in either PHP or the curl extension, but it's easy enough to work around in Guzzle, that's why I am filing this issue here. I expect a faster turnaround time from you than from PHP.

